(5882183, 854747, 18197, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>It looks like this is your second challenge entry. I hope you''ll keep at it. It''s fun, and if you''re like me you''ll learn a ton.<br/> <br/>Now to the photo ... I didn''t vote in this challenge but upon seeing the photo I liked it right away. I get tired of seeing the same old things here, and i appreciated your fresh subject. You did a nice job with focus and depth of field to isolate and showcase your subject and to bring out the various textures. The subject itself is interesting with the little ice-pad-feet for the branch. The composition is good, especially the placement of the branch above midline and the placement of the ice-pad-feet near the rule-of-thirds sweet spots.<br/> <br/>Given the original, it''s hard to imagine how you might process it differently to improve it. Maybe experiment with B&amp;W or Sepia. Maybe try rotating for more of a diagonal composition. I like to experiment with images I like to see if I can improve them.<br/> <br/>When you''re shooting subjects like this, point of view is important. I try to shoot as many angles and perspectives as I can. Perhaps you did this and this is the one you liked best. But if not, try it next time and see if it doesn''t give you more options.<br/> <br/>I look forward to seeing more from you in the future.<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club It looks like this is your second challenge entry. I hope you''ll keep at it. It''s fun, and if you''re like me you''ll learn a ton. Now to the photo ... I didn''t vote in this challenge but upon seeing the photo I liked it right away. I get tired of seeing the same old things here, and i appreciated your fresh subject. You did a nice job with focus and depth of field to isolate and showcase your subject and to bring out the various textures. The subject itself is interesting with the little ice-pad-feet for the branch. The composition is good, especially the placement of the branch above midline and the placement of the ice-pad-feet near the rule-of-thirds sweet spots. Given the original, it''s hard to imagine how you might process it differently to improve it. Maybe experiment with B&W or Sepia. Maybe try rotating for more of a diagonal composition. I like to experiment with images I like to see if I can improve them. When you''re shooting subjects like this, point of view is important. I try to shoot as many angles and perspectives as I can. Perhaps you did this and this is the one you liked best. But if not, try it next time and see if it doesn''t give you more options. I look forward to seeing more from you in the future. ', '2010-02-26 20:50:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(5882189, 853583, 18799, '<td valign="top">Hi from the Critique Club...<br/> <br/>All I can say is this should have been in the 6''s, I think the voters past by too quickly.<br/> <br/>I like the extreme negative space, the little bokeh dots in the background and the creamy transitions.  I think the b/w conversion was spot on as well.<br/> <br/>The only negatives I can find is that there appears to be a slight halo around the ice, probably from sharpening.  It''s very minor though and more of a nit than anything.  The other thing, as long as I''m critiquing ;), is I find the dark line in the center area a bit distracting because of the contrast it makes with the lighter colors.  Maybe a slightly different angle would have eliminated it.   <br/> <br/>Once again, nice shot.</td>', 'Hi from the Critique Club... All I can say is this should have been in the 6''s, I think the voters past by too quickly. I like the extreme negative space, the little bokeh dots in the background and the creamy transitions.  I think the b/w conversion was spot on as well. The only negatives I can find is that there appears to be a slight halo around the ice, probably from sharpening.  It''s very minor though and more of a nit than anything.  The other thing, as long as I''m critiquing ;), is I find the dark line in the center area a bit distracting because of the contrast it makes with the lighter colors.  Maybe a slightly different angle would have eliminated it.    Once again, nice shot.', '2010-02-26 20:56:31.000000', NULL, 0),
