(5891740, 854323, 811, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE<br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>Compositionally, I like how the stone part starts in the left side of the frame and leads through the picture towards the right side.  There is also a nice balance between the two "open" sections.<br/> <br/>Technically, the colors are almost surreal, but in an appealing way.  I like the coolness of the white/blue against the warmness of the orange rocks.  There appears to be some over-sharpness and this could very well be from resizing to fit within dpc parameters.  If you were shooting with a tripod, I am wondering what it would have been like to lower your ISO to as low as it would go, up the f-stop number, and slow the shutter speed down.  This would have slowed some of the motion of the water and it wouldn''t have been as "chunky" and thus wouldn''t have artifacted as bad on resizing.<br/> <br/>Overall a good shot, and a good entry for this particular challenge.<br/> <br/>karma</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUEby karmat Compositionally, I like how the stone part starts in the left side of the frame and leads through the picture towards the right side.  There is also a nice balance between the two "open" sections. Technically, the colors are almost surreal, but in an appealing way.  I like the coolness of the white/blue against the warmness of the orange rocks.  There appears to be some over-sharpness and this could very well be from resizing to fit within dpc parameters.  If you were shooting with a tripod, I am wondering what it would have been like to lower your ISO to as low as it would go, up the f-stop number, and slow the shutter speed down.  This would have slowed some of the motion of the water and it wouldn''t have been as "chunky" and thus wouldn''t have artifacted as bad on resizing. Overall a good shot, and a good entry for this particular challenge. karma', '2010-03-05 16:44:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(5891991, 853926, 81213, '<td valign="top">Here''s your requested critique and feedback! :D<br/> <br/>Very cool waterfall! First off, composition-wise, this photo has no flaws. But when we look at overall quality, I have some things to point out. First, your photos main subject, (the waterfall) is blown-out and overexposed. Bringing your aperture down to about 4 and raising the shutter speed just a tad could have fixed this on-the-spot and gotten the splashing water in perfect, crisp condition. Overexposure is hard to get out in post-processing so this must be done on-camera. Second, the entire photo appears slightly out of focus, maybe it''s just me but I find that to be very bothering. Third, I would like this tons more had there been a person maybe in the waterfall, jumping down or something, just to add a bit more interest to the photo. Overall, it''s not a bad photo, but nor does it really catch my interest.<br/> <br/>Hope that was helpful! :D<br/> <br/>- <img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=81213" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">ColemanGariety</a> <br/>The DPChallenge Critique Club</td>', 'Here''s your requested critique and feedback! :D Very cool waterfall! First off, composition-wise, this photo has no flaws. But when we look at overall quality, I have some things to point out. First, your photos main subject, (the waterfall) is blown-out and overexposed. Bringing your aperture down to about 4 and raising the shutter speed just a tad could have fixed this on-the-spot and gotten the splashing water in perfect, crisp condition. Overexposure is hard to get out in post-processing so this must be done on-camera. Second, the entire photo appears slightly out of focus, maybe it''s just me but I find that to be very bothering. Third, I would like this tons more had there been a person maybe in the waterfall, jumping down or something, just to add a bit more interest to the photo. Overall, it''s not a bad photo, but nor does it really catch my interest. Hope that was helpful! :D -  ColemanGariety The DPChallenge Critique Club', '2010-03-05 21:28:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(5892867, 854715, 106543, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club! </b> <br/> <br/>Sorry about the wait for your critique, this is coming out of the backlog! <br/> <br/>My first impression upon seeing this image is that it was pretty amazing that you captured one rainbow so clearly and that you even managed to get a second in the background, very cool! It looks a little bit over saturated in the yellow and greens but it''s not bad, it really brings out the colours in the rainbow. The only other thing that brought your score down is the title. The other commenters seem to agree that it is a little too long. I wasn''t a fan of it, I gave you a 6 but I might have given a 7 with a better title. I see you''ve entered 16 challenges since you''ve joined. Keep up the great participation!<br/> <br/>If you have any questions <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/messaging.php?action=compose&amp;USER_ID=106543" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">click here to message me</a>! <br/> <br/>Senay <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!   Sorry about the wait for your critique, this is coming out of the backlog!  My first impression upon seeing this image is that it was pretty amazing that you captured one rainbow so clearly and that you even managed to get a second in the background, very cool! It looks a little bit over saturated in the yellow and greens but it''s not bad, it really brings out the colours in the rainbow. The only other thing that brought your score down is the title. The other commenters seem to agree that it is a little too long. I wasn''t a fan of it, I gave you a 6 but I might have given a 7 with a better title. I see you''ve entered 16 challenges since you''ve joined. Keep up the great participation! If you have any questions click here to message me!  Senay ', '2010-03-07 01:21:49.000000', NULL, 0),
(5892873, 854642, 106543, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club! </b> <br/> <br/>Sorry about the wait for your critique, this is coming out of the backlog! <br/> <br/>I feel that the blurred out blinds did help to enhance the image, they give you the perspective of actually being there. I gave you a 7 for this image. The contrast between snow and tree is very striking. The only thing that could be changed would be to add some height. Although the border wouldn''t work as good and the blinds wouldn''t be as good. Taking some space off the sides wouldn''t be good so I guess it''s perfect like this haha. Congratulations on this image making it to your profile. Mine is set to show the top 9 pictures.<br/> <br/>If you have any questions <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/messaging.php?action=compose&amp;USER_ID=106543" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">click here to message me</a>! <br/> <br/>Senay</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!   Sorry about the wait for your critique, this is coming out of the backlog!  I feel that the blurred out blinds did help to enhance the image, they give you the perspective of actually being there. I gave you a 7 for this image. The contrast between snow and tree is very striking. The only thing that could be changed would be to add some height. Although the border wouldn''t work as good and the blinds wouldn''t be as good. Taking some space off the sides wouldn''t be good so I guess it''s perfect like this haha. Congratulations on this image making it to your profile. Mine is set to show the top 9 pictures. If you have any questions click here to message me!  Senay', '2010-03-07 01:36:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(5892884, 854746, 106543, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club! </b> <br/> <br/>Sorry about the wait for your critique, this is coming out of the backlog! <br/> <br/>The first thing I notice right away is the size of your image, challenges can take pictures that are up to 800 pixels wide or high.  It might increase your scores to use as much of that space as you can. It''s a nice sunset picture, I like the sun setting in the distance, the mountains on the far shore and the wave crashing against the rocks. It just seems like it''s missing something, it might just be the grain or it''s a little out of focus or something. For future challenges I suggest looking up how to set the size of your images in whatever program you use for post processing. Best of luck!<br/> <br/>If you have any questions <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/messaging.php?action=compose&amp;USER_ID=106543" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">click here to message me</a>! <br/> <br/>Senay</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!   Sorry about the wait for your critique, this is coming out of the backlog!  The first thing I notice right away is the size of your image, challenges can take pictures that are up to 800 pixels wide or high.  It might increase your scores to use as much of that space as you can. It''s a nice sunset picture, I like the sun setting in the distance, the mountains on the far shore and the wave crashing against the rocks. It just seems like it''s missing something, it might just be the grain or it''s a little out of focus or something. For future challenges I suggest looking up how to set the size of your images in whatever program you use for post processing. Best of luck! If you have any questions click here to message me!  Senay', '2010-03-07 02:01:40.000000', NULL, 0),
(5892886, 854792, 106543, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club! </b> <br/> <br/>Sorry about the wait for your critique, this is coming out of the backlog! <br/> <br/>First off I just want to say that not everyone is a fan of sun flare, I''ve used it in an image and it didn''t do too great. Personally I''m neutral, I''m not going to vote an image down just for having it. Usually it adds some nice colours and gives the picture a cool effect. Like what you did with it here. I gave this a 7 during voting, the only thing I would have liked to see added to this picture would be the tree in full if possible. The meeting of sky and earth through the tree is really nice though, good greens and blues. You finished in the top 50% at least!<br/> <br/>If you have any questions <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/messaging.php?action=compose&amp;USER_ID=106543" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">click here to message me</a>! <br/> <br/>Senay</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!   Sorry about the wait for your critique, this is coming out of the backlog!  First off I just want to say that not everyone is a fan of sun flare, I''ve used it in an image and it didn''t do too great. Personally I''m neutral, I''m not going to vote an image down just for having it. Usually it adds some nice colours and gives the picture a cool effect. Like what you did with it here. I gave this a 7 during voting, the only thing I would have liked to see added to this picture would be the tree in full if possible. The meeting of sky and earth through the tree is really nice though, good greens and blues. You finished in the top 50% at least! If you have any questions click here to message me!  Senay', '2010-03-07 02:12:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(5893797, 854611, 81213, '<td valign="top">Here''s your feedback from the DPChallenge Critique Club! :D<br/> <br/>Wow, this is a great view! I''d love to know where this was taken! The landscape is beautiful, but the lighting in the photo is a bit flat. If you could have captured this with more light shining on the trees and waterfall, it would have a much brighter mood to it. While the snow is overexposed, the waterfall and the trees are very dark and boring. That''s the biggest problem here. Also, there is no directly apparent subject, and the trees take up too much of the photo for there to be one. On the other hand, the waterfall was framed very nicely by the trees and brings this photo from a 4 up to a 4.8. it would also be nice to see either a bit more sky or no sky at all. The little sliver of clouds you have at the top of the photo don''t really do much right now.<br/> <br/>Hope that was helpful to you!<br/> <br/>- <img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=81213" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">ColemanGariety</a> <br/>The DPChallenge Critique Club</td>', 'Here''s your feedback from the DPChallenge Critique Club! :D Wow, this is a great view! I''d love to know where this was taken! The landscape is beautiful, but the lighting in the photo is a bit flat. If you could have captured this with more light shining on the trees and waterfall, it would have a much brighter mood to it. While the snow is overexposed, the waterfall and the trees are very dark and boring. That''s the biggest problem here. Also, there is no directly apparent subject, and the trees take up too much of the photo for there to be one. On the other hand, the waterfall was framed very nicely by the trees and brings this photo from a 4 up to a 4.8. it would also be nice to see either a bit more sky or no sky at all. The little sliver of clouds you have at the top of the photo don''t really do much right now. Hope that was helpful to you! -  ColemanGariety The DPChallenge Critique Club', '2010-03-07 20:31:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(5974137, 854649, 112107, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club ;) <br/> <br/><b>Artistic Merit </b> <br/><i>Pros: </i> <br/>* Very interesting subject. <br/>* Great (mono)tones. <br/>* Good detail. <br/><i>Cons: </i> <br/>* A hard crop would make this work better. Closing in on the "cakes" would make them stand out a bit more from the background. Better yet if you shot it closer as it would help with isolation (see the DOF comment). That green background should never had made it to the final picture.<br/> <br/><b>Technical Merit </b> <br/><i>Pros: </i> <br/>* Spot on exposure. <br/>Cons: <br/>* Not enough isolation. You could have used a much larger aperture. As a consequence of using F9, the background is just too busy. Perhaps you were trying to contrast the delicate subject serendipity with the harshness of the blizzard behind the subject but IMHO it''s barely perceptible. <br/>* Not enough resolution. As commented before you should use the maximum available resolution in you advantage. It could have brought up more details from the subject.<br/> <br/><b>Emotional Merit </b> <br/><i>Pros: </i> <br/>* A good choice of subject. Definitely one of the pictures that best meet the challenge and a very rare sight (it''s a first for me).<br/>* Great constrast between warm tones (the "living" cup) with the cold snow.<br/><i>Cons:</i> <br/>The small resolution image doesn''t have the impact as potential bigger one does.<br/> <br/><b>Result</b> <br/>You probably ended up with a score that was below your expectations but you have to factor in that the average score for this challenge was a monstruous 5.6 which means the entries were of extraordinary high quality. This was also your first and only entry  so I urge you not to be disapointed by this as you are always bound to improve. :)<br/>Your idea was great, you had a great subject but the technical aspects let the photo down. I''m sure you''ll get better as you meet new weekly challenges.<br/> <br/>All the best <br/>Duarte Bruno</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club ;)  Artistic Merit  Pros:  * Very interesting subject. * Great (mono)tones. * Good detail. Cons:  * A hard crop would make this work better. Closing in on the "cakes" would make them stand out a bit more from the background. Better yet if you shot it closer as it would help with isolation (see the DOF comment). That green background should never had made it to the final picture. Technical Merit  Pros:  * Spot on exposure. Cons: * Not enough isolation. You could have used a much larger aperture. As a consequence of using F9, the background is just too busy. Perhaps you were trying to contrast the delicate subject serendipity with the harshness of the blizzard behind the subject but IMHO it''s barely perceptible. * Not enough resolution. As commented before you should use the maximum available resolution in you advantage. It could have brought up more details from the subject. Emotional Merit  Pros:  * A good choice of subject. Definitely one of the pictures that best meet the challenge and a very rare sight (it''s a first for me).* Great constrast between warm tones (the "living" cup) with the cold snow.Cons: The small resolution image doesn''t have the impact as potential bigger one does. Result You probably ended up with a score that was below your expectations but you have to factor in that the average score for this challenge was a monstruous 5.6 which means the entries were of extraordinary high quality. This was also your first and only entry  so I urge you not to be disapointed by this as you are always bound to improve. :)Your idea was great, you had a great subject but the technical aspects let the photo down. I''m sure you''ll get better as you meet new weekly challenges. All the best Duarte Bruno', '2010-04-27 11:51:36.000000', NULL, 0),
